# Defence in depth

### Walls, policies and poka-yoke in rung 6

Week 10, rung 6 of 7 (Guarded), talk 2

---

## Walls, one behind the other

```
course Markdown ──► escaping ──► sanitising ──► page ──► CSP in the browser
                     (rung 2)     (rung 6)                (rung 6)
course folder  ──► containedPath in every adapter ──► gen
course settings ─► a pure policy that fails closed ──► who may read
npm registry   ──► lockfile, registry sources, audit ──► what we install
```

Each wall assumes the one before it can fail. With depth, a bug in one wall is a finding, not a breach.

---

## Escaping is not sanitising

**Escaping** changes how text is *written* so it stays text: `<` becomes `&lt;`.
**Sanitising** decides whether a value is *allowed at all*.

Rungs 2 to 5 escape everything. This still works:

```html
<a href="javascript:alert(document.domain)">the reading list</a>
```

No character in it needs escaping. The browser runs it when a reader clicks. Rung 5's
`packages/lite/src/markdown.ts` says so: "sanitising links such as `javascript:` is rung 6 (Guarded)".

---

## Allow-list, never block-list

`packages/lite/src/url.ts` (R-022):

```ts
const ALLOWED: Record<UrlUse, ReadonlySet<string>> = {
  link: new Set(["http", "https", "mailto"]),
  image: new Set(["http", "https"]),
};

export function safeUrl(url: string, use: UrlUse): string | null {
  const read = url.replace(/[\u0000- \u007f-\u009f]/g, "").toLowerCase();
  const scheme = read.match(/^([a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*):/)?.[1];
  if (scheme !== undefined) return ALLOWED[use].has(scheme) ? url : null;
  const colon = read.indexOf(":");
  const pathStart = read.search(/[/?#]/);
  if (colon !== -1 && (pathStart === -1 || colon < pathStart)) return null;
  return url;
}
```

A block-list of `javascript:` misses `JaVaScRiPt:`, `java\tscript:`, `vbscript:` and next year's trick.

---

## Read the address the way a browser does

| Payload | Why a naive filter misses it |
| --- | --- |
| `JaVaScRiPt:alert(1)` | schemes are case-insensitive |
| `java\tscript:alert(1)` | browsers delete tabs and newlines inside a URL |
| `\u0001javascript:alert(1)` | and strip leading control characters |
| `&#106;avascript:alert(1)` | an entity; escaping makes it harmless text, the allow-list refuses it anyway |
| `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` | an SVG can carry an `onload` |

A refused address is shown as its text in `<span class="blocked">`, so the page still reads. The reader
uses the same function, and checks every route as `tutors.json` enters (R-033).

---

## Proving a sanitiser: corpus, property, browser

- **A corpus** (`tests/unit/lite/xss-corpus.test.ts`): 15 hostile addresses and 11 hostile texts, each with
  the reason it is dangerous, through every Markdown construct and every field of every kind
- **A property** (`tests/fuzz/safe-url.fuzz.test.ts`): fast-check builds addresses the way an attacker does;
  a block-list is the negative fixture that proves the property can fail
- **An independent oracle** (`tests/support/xss-oracle.ts`): Node's WHATWG `new URL(...)` decides the real
  scheme, not lite's own code
- **A browser** (`tests/e2e/guarded.e2e.ts`): the hostile course, opened in Chromium, runs no script

---

## A Teaching Failure from the corpus

Take `safeUrl` out of `markdown.ts` and the gate says:

```
XSS corpus: "JaVaScRiPt:alert(1)" (schemes are case-insensitive) reached the page
as href="JaVaScRiPt:alert(1" opens as javascript:.
Course text must be escaped (html`` in packages/lite/src/html.ts) and every course
URL must pass safeUrl (packages/lite/src/url.ts) before it reaches an href or src.
```

The payload, why it matters, what reached the page and the file to fix: L4, Explained. Nobody has to
relearn this attack to fix it.

---

## The last wall: Content Security Policy

`packages/lite/src/html.ts` (R-023):

```
default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' https:; style-src 'sha256-uFXD...';
base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'
```

- `default-src 'none'` and no `script-src`: **no script runs at all**, inline or loaded
- the one inline `<style>` is allowed by its SHA-256 hash, never `'unsafe-inline'`
- `base-uri 'none'` stops an injected `<base>` re-pointing every relative link

A meta tag cannot set `frame-ancestors`; that needs a response header from the host.

---

## A CSP for an app that is a script

The reader cannot say "no script". `apps/reader/csp.ts` (R-034):

```ts
export const READER_CSP = {
  "default-src": ["none"],
  "script-src": ["self"],
  "style-src": ["self"],
  "style-src-attr": ["unsafe-hashes", ANNOUNCER_STYLE_HASH],
  "img-src": ["self", "https:"],
  "connect-src": ["self"],
  ...
```

SvelteKit hashes its own inline start-up script (`csp: { mode: "hash" }`). Its route announcer hides itself
with a `style` attribute, allowed by one hash that a scenario recomputes from SvelteKit's source. Upstream
has not shipped a CSP yet: the reader loads Iconify, KaTeX, Mermaid, embeds and Supabase realtime.

---

## Containment at the port

`packages/gen/src/file-system-port.ts` (R-024): `containedPath` refuses `..`, `/abs`, `C:/abs`, `~/x` and
NUL bytes. `NodeFileSystem` also resolves the real path:

```ts
const full = join(this.root, containedPath(path));
const real = await realpath(full).catch(() => undefined);
...
if (real !== root && !real.startsWith(root + sep))
  throw new PathEscapeError(path, "a link leads outside the course folder");
```

- The adapter is where a path meets real files, so a new caller cannot skip the check
- The Containment rule: every class that `implements FileSystemPort` calls `containedPath`
- `MemoryFileSystem` holds links too, and the same contract cases run on both: an **Honest Double**

---

## Authentication is not authorisation

- **Authentication**: who is this? A verified identity, from a provider such as GitHub
- **Authorisation**: what may they do? A policy over that identity and the thing they want

| Concern | Upstream | Rung 6 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Verified identity | `packages/jsr/identity` | `packages/identity` |
| Provider adapter | `packages/svelte/identity-sveltekit` (Better Auth) | none: nothing here signs anyone in |
| Roles and permissions | `packages/svelte/utils/rbac` | `packages/rbac` |

Upstream's rule `identity-contracts-self-contained` (`.dependency-cruiser.cjs`): identity imports nothing.
Rung 6's Identity rule says the same, with a teaching message.

---

## Authorisation as a pure policy

`packages/rbac/src/index.ts` (R-030, R-031):

```ts
export function canView(actor: Actor | null, access: CourseAccess): Decision {
  const role = roleOf(actor, access);
  if (isPublic(access)) return { allowed: true, role };
  if (actor === null) return { allowed: false, role, reason: "sign-in-required" };
  if (role === "educator" || access.whitelist.length === 0 || access.whitelist.includes(actor.login))
    return { allowed: true, role };
  return { allowed: false, role, reason: "not-enrolled" };
}
```

The Purity rule: no async, no clock, no state, `import type` only. So `tests/unit/rbac/policy.test.ts` can
be a table of every role against every kind of course, and the table is the whole truth.

---

## Failing closed

```ts
// accessOf: anything that is not a whole number 0 or above reads as 1
const level = typeof auth === "number" && Number.isInteger(auth) && auth >= 0 ? auth : 1;
```

`auth: yes`, `auth: "1"`, `auth: true` and `auth: -1` all make a course **private**. A typo should cost a
sign-in, not leak a course.

- The command refuses a private course unless you pass `--private` (R-028); then every page is marked
- The reader answers an anonymous reader with a 403 notice (R-032). That is a **courtesy, not a lock**: the
  files are still on the host. The host, and upstream's Row-Level Security, are the lock

---

## The supply chain

`scripts/supply-chain.ts`, offline in the Single Gate (R-035, R-036), online on demand:

| Rule | Finding |
| --- | --- |
| a lockfile exists | `lockfile-missing` |
| every dependency is `workspace:*` or a registry version | `non-registry-source` |
| every allowance has a reason and an unexpired date | `malformed-allowance`, `expired-allowance` |
| `pnpm audit:online`: every advisory is allowed | `advisory` |

Upstream: `scripts/checks/dependency-audit.ts`, `tests/security/audit-allowlist.json`, CodeQL, zizmor and
Scorecard workflows that start from `permissions: {}`, and `release/openvex.json`. An allowance with an
expiry and a VEX statement with a justification share one idea: **an exception says why, and can be checked**.

---

## Poka-yoke: mistake-proofing as L5

Lean's answer to defects that escape: make the mistake impossible.

| Poka-yoke in rung 6 | The mistake it removes |
| --- | --- |
| `buildSite(course, { access })`: `access` is required | building a private course as if it were public: `tsc` says `Expected 2 arguments, but got 1` |
| `html` returns `SafeHtml`; only two files may construct one | forgetting to escape |
| `Record<Role, readonly Permission[]>` | a new role with no permissions line |
| the browser enforcing the CSP | any script that slipped past every test |

A check (L3) finds a defect after it is made. A poka-yoke (L5) means it is never made.

---

## Not every rule can be L5

| Rule | Level | Why not higher |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Every course URL passes the allow-list | L3/L4 | a type cannot know a string came from a course; the corpus and two architecture rules check it |
| Allow-list, never block-list | L2 | a habit, in `AGENTS.md` |
| No advisory without an allowance | L3, online only | a gate that turns red when the network does is a gate people learn to ignore |
| A new allowance, a weaker CSP | a person | the Human Boundary: "Ask a human first" |

Choosing the level is this week's Evaluate work: what does an escape cost, and what does the check cost?

---

## Five Whys on an XSS escape

A `javascript:` link reached a rung 5 page.

1. Why did it run? The page held it as an `href`.
2. Why? `renderInline` wrote the author's address into the `href`.
3. Why? It escaped the address, and escaping changes no character of it.
4. Why was that thought enough? The only rule was R-008, "the site shall escape ...", and it passed.
5. Why was there no rule about addresses? Nobody had named the author as an adversary.

The root cause is a missing requirement, not a missing line. The countermeasure is R-022 and its corpus,
then a CSP behind it. You do this for real in the lab.

---

## Summary

| Wall | Stops | Proved by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Escaping | text becoming markup | the corpus, text half; `SafeHtml` |
| Sanitising | script-bearing addresses | the corpus, the property, the oracle |
| CSP | whatever slips past both | hash checks, and Chromium |
| Containment | reading outside the course | the port contract on both adapters |
| Policy | readers who should not read | a table; fail closed |
| Supply chain | what we did not mean to install | the audit, offline and online |
